+## Follow-up pass on 22 November
+
+A review of the 21 November pass asked for three things: a worked example behind the SDK rounding claim, the commit the pass started from, and tests of the code examples. Building the example showed the rounding claim was wrong. This pass corrects it, fixes the follow-ups the 21 November pass listed, and records how the examples were tested.
+
+### Corrections to the 21 November pass
+
+- **[Code demos, Swap (base-input)](/products/cpmm/code-demos#swap-base-input):** how far the SDK quote can be from the program's. When the creator fee is on the input, the program rounds the combined trade and creator fee up once; the SDK rounds each fee up separately. The SDK's fee can therefore be one input unit higher, and one input unit can be worth many output units. For example, with 1,000 input units on tier 0 (0.25% trade fee, 0.05% creator fee), the SDK takes a fee of 4 and the program 3. With equal reserves the SDK quotes 1 unit less; with reserves of 10⁹ input to 10¹⁵ output it quotes 999,998 units less. Source: [calculator.rs L108–118](https://github.com/raydium-io/raydium-cp-swap/blob/3d69122790e97cd6aff24e8ba95940793773f58c/programs/cp-swap/src/curve/calculator.rs#L108-L118) · [SDK curve/calculator.ts L56–64](https://github.com/raydium-io/raydium-sdk-V2/blob/32a184eb0aea15ddbb485e24a1a73d3723fd94d6/src/raydium/cpmm/curve/calculator.ts#L56-L64)
+  - **Was:** "it rounds the input-side creator and trade fees separately, so its output can be one unit below the program's."
+  - **Now:** "When the creator fee is on the input, it rounds the trade and creator fees up separately, while the program rounds their sum once. The SDK's fee can therefore be one input unit higher, and its quote lower than the program pays by that unit's worth of output, which can be many output units."
