+## Follow-up pass on 22 November
+
+A review of the 21 November pass asked for execution evidence behind the Farm v6 claim path and the LaunchLab vesting start, a corrected page inventory, and saved evidence for the UI and API values. The LaunchLab pages also disagreed with each other: the vesting page put the start at fundraising completion and the instructions page at migration. An uncommitted edit between the passes had settled this in favour of migration. This pass checks each point against mainnet transactions, the SDK or the production UI, corrects what was wrong, and saves the evidence. The farm claim path turned out to be wrong on every farm version, not only v6.
+
+### Farm claim path
+
+- **[Claim rewards](/user-flows/claim-rewards#farm-harvest), [Add or remove liquidity](/user-flows/add-remove-liquidity#harvest-farm-rewards), [Farm instructions](/products/farm-staking/instructions#instruction-inventory) and [Farm overview](/products/farm-staking/overview):** the 21 November pass said Farm v6 has an explicit `Harvest` instruction, and that legacy v3 and v5 claim with `Deposit` and `amount = 0`. The SDK and mainnet both claim with `Withdraw` and `amount = 0` on every version. Sources: [`harvestAllRewards`, farm.ts L964–982](https://github.com/raydium-io/raydium-sdk-V2/blob/32a184eb0aea15ddbb485e24a1a73d3723fd94d6/src/raydium/farm/farm.ts#L964-L982) · [`makeWithdrawInstructionV6`, instruction.ts L838–876](https://github.com/raydium-io/raydium-sdk-V2/blob/32a184eb0aea15ddbb485e24a1a73d3723fd94d6/src/raydium/farm/instruction.ts#L838-L876)
+  - **Was:** "Farm v6 has an explicit `Harvest` instruction. Legacy Farm v3 and v5 instead use `Deposit` with `amount = 0` to settle rewards without changing stake." The farm instruction page had a `Harvest` (v6) section that took no arguments.
+  - **Now:** "**Harvest** is the UI action. On Farm v6 and on legacy Farm v3 and v5, the SDK behind it sends `Withdraw` with `amount = 0`, which settles rewards without changing your stake." The farm instruction page replaces the `Harvest` section with "Claim-only settlement".
+  - **Evidence:** `scripts/check-farm-claims.py` decoded the 1,000 most recent successful transactions of each farm program by the SDK's instruction tags:
+
+    | Program | Window | `Withdraw`, amount 0 | `Deposit`, amount 0 | Other calls |
+    |---|---|---|---|---|
+    | Farm v6 | 15 Oct – 22 Nov 2026 | 563 | 0 | 372 stakes, 235 unstakes, 89 farm-creator calls |
+    | Farm v5 | 23 Jan – 22 Nov 2026 | 490 | 3 | 322 unstakes, 57 ledger creations, 606 calls with tag 2, which the SDK does not build |
+    | Farm v3 | 17 – 22 Nov 2026 | 424 | 0 | 433 stakes, 143 unstakes, 71 ledger creations |
+
+    The Farm v6 sample contained no instruction outside the SDK's six. The evidence is in `public/tino-changes/evidence/farm-claims-2026-11-22/`.
+- **[Rust CPI, Farm v6](/sdk-api/rust-cpi#farm-v6), [CPI integration](/integration-guides/cpi-integration#remaining-accounts) and the [home page](/docs/en):** these described a separate harvest call, and Rust CPI gave an account order that does not match a real claim. The SDK builds a 9-byte `Withdraw`: the tag and a zero `u64`. In three saved claims, the fourth account is the farm's LP vault: it holds the same mint as the caller's LP account, seventh, and is owned by the farm authority. The reward pairs start with the first reward.
+  - **Was:** "For the zero-argument instruction shape consistent with a harvest or claim call, the real account order is a fixed prefix (`token_program`, the farm's state account, a vault-authority PDA, that PDA's first reward vault, a second PDA, the caller, and the caller's ATA for that first reward mint), followed by `(reward_vault_i, user_reward_ata_i)` pairs in `remaining_accounts` for every reward stream after the first." CPI integration listed "Farm v6 `Deposit` / `Harvest` / `Withdraw`", and the home page's farm card listed `harvest` as an instruction.
+  - **Now:** "Seven fixed accounts come first: `token_program`, the farm's state account, the farm authority PDA, the farm's LP vault, the caller's ledger PDA, the caller (signer), and the caller's LP token account. Then come `(reward_vault_i, user_reward_ata_i)` pairs, one per reward stream, starting with the first." The home page card lists `createFarm · deposit · withdraw`.
+
+### LaunchLab vesting clock start
+
+- **Evidence:** the IDL's `PoolState` has no timestamp besides `vesting_schedule.start_time`. Whichever instruction writes it therefore does so at block time `start_time − cliff_period`. `scripts/check-launchlab-vesting.py` read all 18,902 migrated LaunchLab pools and took the 12 most recent of the 102 that lock tokens with a cliff. For each, it decoded the transactions within 60 seconds of that block time, including the `pool_status` each trade event reports. It found the completing buy, the first trade whose event reports `Migrate`, in 10 pools:
+  - In 9, the completing buy's block time equals `start_time − cliff_period`.
+  - In 8, migration landed in a later slot. In 3 of those it also landed in a later second, 1 to 9 seconds after the clock start, so migration cannot have set the clock.
+  - In the tenth, the buy and the migration share a slot one second after `start_time − cliff_period`, which fits neither reading exactly.
+  - The other 2 pools had no transaction within 60 seconds of the clock start in the RPC's history.
+
+  The evidence is in `public/tino-changes/evidence/launchlab-vesting-2026-11-22/result.json`.
+- **[Vesting](/products/launchlab/vesting), [accounts](/products/launchlab/accounts) and [Creating a LaunchLab token](/user-flows/creating-a-launchlab-token#vesting):** keep fundraising completion as the start, and name the instruction that sets it.
+  - **Was:** "These three values live on `PoolState.vesting_schedule` (a `VestingSchedule` struct) plus the on-chain `start_time`, which the program records as `block_time + cliff_period` at the moment fundraising successfully ends (when graduation conditions are first met)."
+  - **Now:** "The buy that completes fundraising records `start_time` as its own `block_time + cliff_period`. The later `MigrateToCpswap` transaction does not change it, so a delayed migration does not delay the unlock schedule."
+- **[LaunchLab instructions, `Buy`](/products/launchlab/instructions#buy-canonical-variant-buyexactin):** said a separate `Graduate` instruction ends the curve. The IDL has no such instruction, and the completing buy's own trade event reports `Migrate`.
+  - **Was:** "If `quote_reserve_real ≥ quote_reserve_target` after the update, the SDK typically chains a `Graduate` ix in the same transaction. The program does not auto-graduate inside `Buy` — a subsequent `Graduate` is required."
+  - **Now:** "If this buy completes fundraising, the program moves `pool_state.status` to `Migrate` and sets `vesting_schedule.start_time = block_time + cliff_period` in the same instruction. There is no separate graduate instruction."
+- **[LaunchLab instructions, migration](/products/launchlab/instructions#migratetoamm-/-migratetocpswap) and the state tables on [instructions](/products/launchlab/instructions#state-change-matrix) and [accounts](/products/launchlab/accounts#lifecycle-account-transitions):** had migration set `start_time`, and used a `Migrated` status that the IDL's `PoolStatus` (`Fund`, `Migrate`, `Trade`) does not have.
+  - **Was:** "Flip `pool_state.status = Migrated`, set `vesting_schedule.start_time = block_time + cliff_period`." The accounts table showed migration as "Funding → Migrate → Migrated".
+  - **Now:** "Set `pool_state.status = Trade`. `vesting_schedule.start_time` is left unchanged: the buy that completed fundraising already set it." Both tables use `Fund`, `Migrate` and `Trade`, with the completing buy as the `Fund → Migrate` step.
